--
N5008 [temp.constr.order]/1 + [over.match.best]/2.6: of two otherwise
indistinguishable function-template overloads, the more-constrained is chosen.
Cheap subsumes Cheap||Rare, so f<Cheap> must win for an int argument (g++
returns 1).

KNOWNBUG: CBMC selects the less-constrained f<CheapOrRare> and returns 2.

Root cause (traced): the two overloads have identical signatures `f(T) -> int`,
differing only in concept constraint, and their int instantiations collide on a
single instance symbol f<signed_int>(signed_int); only one candidate reaches
overload disambiguation, so constraint-subsumption ordering never runs.  Fixing
this needs both (a) same-signature concept-constrained overloads forming a
proper overload set (distinct instances) and (b) subsumption ordering
([temp.constr.order]/1: normalise to atomic constraints, P subsumes Q iff every
disjunctive clause of P meets every conjunctive clause of Q).  Flip to CORE once
the more-constrained overload wins: assertion 1 SUCCESS, assertion 2 (a wrong
value) FAILURE.
